            <ArticleTitle>Emergence of Ceftolozane-Tazobactam-Resistant Pseudomonas aeruginosa during Treatment Is Mediated by a Single AmpC Structural Mutation.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">e01183-17</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1128/AAC.01183-17</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Ceftolozane-tazobactam is a cephalosporin-β-lactamase inhibitor combination that exhibits potent in vitro activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa, including strains that are resistant to other β-lactams. The emergence of ceftolozane-tazobactam resistance among clinical isolates of P. aeruginosa has rarely been described. Here we characterized ceftolozane-tazobactam-resistant P. aeruginosa strains recovered from a patient who was treated with this agent for 6 weeks for a recurrent wound infection. The results showed that the resistance was mediated by a single AmpC structural mutation.</AbstractText>
